                <pb facs="0432" n="424" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg424"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/11843: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Consul at Tsingtao</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Sokobin</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-12-28T19:00:00-05:00">December 28, 1937—7
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your December 27, 4 p.m. On the assumption that it was your intention simply to
                    indicate to the Senior Consul that the matter of Americans acting as “special
                    constables” was one which you were not called upon to approve or disapprove, the
                    Department feels that you might have added that although you were continuing to
                    urge all Americans to withdraw from Tsingtao you of course would not wish to
                    interpose any objection or obstacle to appropriate provisional measures which
                    Americans and other foreigners who elected to remain in Tsingtao might find it
                    desirable to adopt for their self-protection during a temporary period of
                    emergency.</p>
                <p>Please repeat to Hankow and Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
